# Meta Pixel: ask which one before onboarding a tenant

`template_locations.meta_pixel_ids` is **not** a copy-paste field. We run two
separate Meta datasets (business Mysite AI) and putting a tenant in the wrong
    10|one silently poisons audiences and lookalikes on the other.

| Dataset | ID | For |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `RestaurantsWebPixel` | `849479710958676` | Gastro — restaurants, cafés, bars, pizzerias, food trucks, sushi, catering |
| `RetailWebPixel` | `4347830552199286` | Brick-and-mortar retail / services **outside** gastro — pet stores, print shops, salons, bike shops |

## The rule

When creating or configuring a tenant, **ask the operator which pixel applies**
before writing `meta_pixel_ids`. Do not infer it from the tenant's name — "Guido's
    20|Pizza" is obvious, "Lindley" and "Shaddai" are not, and both were initially
onboarded onto the restaurant pixel by mistake (fixed in migration `043`).

Use `AskQuestion` with the two options above, and default the recommendation to
whichever category the tenant's `about_md` / menu presence suggests — but let the
operator confirm.

If the answer is genuinely "neither" (tenant doesn't run Meta ads), write
`'{}'::text[]` — the empty array is a real, supported state. `BaseLayout` then
skips the pixel island entirely, so the tenant ships no `fbevents.js` at all.
    30|Roughly 50 unpublished/inactive tenants sit in this state on purpose.

## Choosing

- Retail = the guest buys a **product** and leaves. No table, no menu, no service window.
- Gastro = the guest consumes **on premises or via delivery/pickup of prepared food**.
- Edge cases (bakery with seating, brewery taproom, deli): treat as gastro if
  there's a menu of prepared items. Ask if unsure.

## Writing it

    40|```sql
-- Gastro
insert into template_locations (..., meta_pixel_ids)
values (..., array['849479710958676']::text[]);

-- Retail
update template_locations l set meta_pixel_ids = array['4347830552199286']::text[]
  from template_brands b where l.brand_id = b.id and b.slug = 'my-pet-store';
```

    50|**Replace, don't append.** Separation is the whole point of the second dataset.
Listing both ids is supported (`MetaPixel.tsx` inits every entry in order) but is
an explicit exception — call it out to the operator rather than doing it quietly.

Never hardcode either ID in `src/` — the values live in the DB only, so a pixel
swap never needs a deploy. Both IDs appear in this rule and in migration `043`;
that's the only place they belong outside Supabase.
